  hiot:firmwareUpdateModel hiot:OtaAutomatic ;
  hiot:consumerAvailability hiot:RetailConsumer ;
  hiot:actuationConsequence hiot:NoActuation ;
  hiot:dataSensitivity hiot:AvStreamData ;
  hiot:adminModel hiot:AppOnlyAdmin, hiot:CloudPortalAdmin ;
  hiot:credentialModel hiot:AccountBound ;
  hiot:patchResponsibility hiot:VendorPatched ;
  hiot:supportLifetime hiot:DeclaredLifetime ;
  hiot:resolvedScopeCall """RESOLVED 2026-08-04 — smart displays stay MERGED here, not split out. Evidence: of 38 confirmed-Yes rows only 2 touch a display product (a Lenovo Smart Assistant app and a Lenovo Smart Clock); the category's real content is Sonos, Bose SoundTouch, Echo Dot and Xiaomi speakers. Independently, term_precision.csv scores the term `smart display` at 48 judged / 0 Yes (prune_candidate) in BOTH the vendor and keyword blocks, and `smart screen` at 1/0. A split would yield n~2 — the same tail problem the folding categories exist to fix. Smart displays are in scope by definition but have essentially no distinct NVD footprint.""" ;
  skos:scopeNote """IN: consumer smart speakers, smart displays, and smart soundbars (Echo, Nest, HomePod) — qualify as home-control surfaces via criterion 4(b). OUT: plain Bluetooth speakers, headphones, and AV receivers with no assistant/home-control role.""" .

hiot:sleeptracker a owl:Class, hiot:DeviceType ;
  rdfs:subClassOf hiot:SleepDevice ;
  hiot:slug "sleeptracker" ;
  rdfs:label "sleep tracker" ;
  hiot:sortOrder 2 ;
  hiot:hasConnectivity hiot:wifi, hiot:ble ;
  hiot:hasDeviceClass hiot:EmbeddedSensor ;
  hiot:hasDeployment hiot:Residential ;
  hiot:hasFunction hiot:Monitor ;
  hiot:hasSecurityContext hiot:ConsumerManaged ;
  hiot:formFactor hiot:Fixed ;
  hiot:placement hiot:Indoor ;
  hiot:actuatesPhysical false ;
  hiot:capturesAV false ;
  hiot:cloudDependence hiot:CloudRequired ;
  hiot:topology hiot:DirectIP ;
  hiot:pairingModel hiot:BleProvisioned, hiot:AccountLinked ;
  hiot:computeTier hiot:McuClass ;
  hiot:hasWebAdminUI false ;
  hiot:firmwareUpdateModel hiot:OtaAutomatic ;
  hiot:consumerAvailability hiot:RetailConsumer ;
  hiot:actuationConsequence hiot:NoActuation ;
  hiot:dataSensitivity hiot:BiometricData ;
  hiot:adminModel hiot:AppOnlyAdmin ;
  hiot:credentialModel hiot:AccountBound ;
  hiot:patchResponsibility hiot:VendorPatched ;
  hiot:supportLifetime hiot:UndeclaredLifetime ;
  hiot:provisional true ;
  hiot:noNvdFootprint true ;
  rdfs:comment """RESOLVED 2026-08-04 — NOT dropped; reclassified as definitionally in scope but operationally invisible to NVD (see hiot:noNvdFootprint).

Evidence: 0 confirmed-Yes from 29 judged rows. Critically this is NOT a term problem — the keyword terms are correct and DID fire (`bedside monitor`, `under-mattress sensor`, `smart mattress`, `contactless sleep monitor`, `cpap`; the cpap term returned a BMC Medical Luna CPAP machine, judged No as a medical device). The ~88% wrist-wearable contamination is out by criterion 3, and what remains has no NVD footprint at all.

Dropping the category would take the frozen scope from 24 to 23, break categories.csv, and contradict the paper text. Marking it instead uses the distinction the paper already draws in sec:method-scope between the five definitional criteria and the operational study-inclusion criterion. Precedent: game consoles are defined-then-excluded too, but they fail definitional criteria 2 and 4, which is why they carry hiot:failsCriteria and this does not.""" ;
  skos:scopeNote """IN: bedside / non-wearable sleep monitors. OUT: wrist wearables and fitness trackers (Fitbit, Apple Watch, Garmin).""" .

